well one of the issues we're talking  about there was this decision to  extend the gap between vaccine doses up  to 12 weeks now it does  worry several groups including the  doctors association and we can talk to  uh that one of their members and gp  rosie shire good to see you this morning  look  the health secretary there saying you  know they'd rather give people sort of  50  protection and get more people out there  protected rather than get  everyone doubled up with their doses why  is a plan does  not that not work for you good morning  steven um  on behalf of the doctors association uk  thank you for having me on the program  um we're really concerned by this  decision to  extend the vaccination schedule for the  pfizer vaccination  from what was studied uh when they  designed the vaccination  which was that people would have two  doses of the fisa vaccination  three weeks apart and that showed that  it gave them  90 percent immunity to then  extend this to give them two doses 12  weeks apart  and what really concerns us is we don't  know what happens if you don't give  that second dose of vaccination after  three weeks  and yet the i mean you're quite right  and  in a sense no one is claiming at this  stage that they do know but the health  secretary  and some others including some of the  scientific advisors saying well  even with that uncertainty to get people  some level of immunity is more important  it would be great if we could say yes  they're going to have some level of  immunity  that will continue after that three week  days but think about the worst case  scenario that after three weeks  that immunity just wanes completely or  maybe  if we then give the booster at 12 weeks  that's just not good enough  the fact is that people are being  vaccinated now and they're being put  into  what is effectively an unregulated  unlicensed  trial whereby they're receiving this  vaccination  on the understanding that then they  don't know what's going on  and on the ground that's really hard for  us as  gps as vaccinators to have to explain  that to people so that they fully  understand the implications of what's  going on  and if we give them the oxford  vaccination we can say to them  you will get your second dose in 12  weeks time and that's what the study  did and they show that you get this  level of immunity after that  with the fisa vaccination when we give  that we're having to say to people  you're going to get some immunity and we  know  we don't know what's going to happen  after that three week period  because it hasn't been looked at and  you're going to have to then  wait till 12 weeks and we hope it's  going to be 12 weeks because the rules  were suddenly changed  when in december when we first started  vaccinating we told these people you  were getting your second dose in three  weeks  and suddenly we had to cancel all these  second doses  so we just need more information to be  able to tell people  so that they understand the risk that  we're having to to go through at the  moment  but the chief medical officer chris  whittie has said you know this is a  it's a public health decision and he  thinks it's a reasonable thing to do  because in his words our major  limitation  of the number of vaccines we actually  have to give it's not the ability to  vaccinate it's a number of vaccines  that have to be given so in terms of  public health  he thinks it's worth the risk they've  obviously looked into this and that's  really  it's reassuring to know i think that  they have looked at it but we don't know  all the data around this we don't know  what's been considered  and we're the ones on the ground who are  actually talking to people about the  vaccination  and we're the ones that are having to  take the consent and when we talk about  consent we always talk about informed  consent so people understand  the risks and the benefits to a  procedure or a vaccination  and i also think it's really important  just to mention here that we're hearing  a lot of statistics about  three quarters of over 80s have been  vaccinated well actually no they haven't  been  three quarters of over 80 year olds have  received their first  dose of the vaccination and they're not  going to be fully vaccinated  until they've received that second dose  and if they've had the fisa vaccination  we don't know if they are going to be  fully vaccinated when they receive the  second dose  because that schedule has been changed  from what was studied  and what was licensed is there a  logistical issue from a sort of gp  perspective in keeping track of people  over you know a minimum of a three-month  period to get them back in for a second  dose  so at the moment everything is recorded  onto a system called pinnacle and then  that gets  uploaded onto the patient's records it  is a potential logistical problem of  having to phone people again  when we initially gave the fisa in  december we could say to people  this is your fisa vaccination please  come back in three weeks time  same time same place and you'll get your  second vaccination  and that was great that was really easy  for people to understand and it didn't  take  the the manpower the people on the  phones phoning people up  now with this three-month gap we're  going to have to get everybody back on  the phone  phoning all those five million people to  say please can you come back  to have your second vaccination at this  time this place  um another issue we're having is knowing  what vaccinations we're getting and when  we're getting them  and we're not seeing much transparency  about the delivery schedule and the  supply chains  um so we can only book people in a week  in advance  if we knew more what we were going to  get and we could have guarantees of how  many vaccinations we were going to get  and also which type of vaccination we  were going to get we would be able to  plan much more efficiently  and hopefully increase the vaccinating  dr good to talk to you this morning  thank you well

